<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ouro Preto</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Vila Rica, Vila Rica de Ouro Preto</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ouro Preto, formerly Vila Rica, is a town in the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, founded during the gold rush of the late 17th and early 18th centuries. It was the capital of the Captaincy of Minas Gerais and the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inconfidencia-mineira">Inconfidência Mineira</a> conspiracy of 1789. Its baroque churches, including works by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleijadinho">Aleijadinho</a>, made it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1980.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ouro-preto.jpg" alt="Ouro Preto" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raquel Mendes Silva · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conjunto_arquitet%C3%B4nico_e_urban%C3%ADstico_de_Ouro_Preto.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the end of the seventeenth century the bandeirantes found gold in Minas Gerais, and in the first half of the eighteenth century a rush of people from the colony and from Portugal built <b>Vila Rica de Ouro Preto</b>, Sabará and Mariana from 1711.</blockquote><a href="/empire/colonial-brazil">Colonial Brazil · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After a tax revolt at <b>Vila Rica</b> in 1720 led by Filipe dos Santos, the crown separated the mining region from the Captaincy of São Paulo as its own captaincy, with Vila Rica, now Ouro Preto, as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-minas-gerais">Captaincy of Minas Gerais</a></li></ul></section>
